== named-list-lacks: a named list `[(=h) = rx]`, the argument lacks h, has it behind a wall, or behind a gate
   program `N(Ae) SEQ N(Aw) SEQ N(Ag)`, Ae := /(?<e>\d)/; Aw := [_w = /(?<h>\d)/]; Q := /(?<h>\d)/; Ag := /${Q}/; N(rx) := /${[(=h) = rx]}xx/
   hand: his lean: each call is a USE error at the call (Ae exports no h; Aw keeps it behind `_w`; Ag uses Q alone, exporting nothing): the error names the call and the argument; N's definition is legal

== named-list-nested: his use error when the named-list macro is called inside another body
   program `M(Ae) & (M.h: <5)`, Ae := /(?<e>\d)/; M(r) := /<${[(=) = N(r)]}>/; N(rx) := /${[(=h) = rx]}xx/
   read M.h
      now  unparsed: label [(=h) = rx]
      A0   unparsed: label [(=h) = rx]
      A    unparsed: label [(=h) = rx]
      A2   unparsed: label [(=h) = rx]
      B    unparsed: label [(=h) = rx]
   hand: M's definition cannot be judged (r is unknown there), so the error surfaces at `M(Ae)`, two bodies away from the list that requires h; the message must name N's list, M's place and Ae. With M2(r) := /<${N([q = r])}>/ the candidate DECIDES it at M2's definition: `[q = r]` exports only q (the place of r passes nothing through q), so `(=h)` can never be met: a definition error after all

